  "unit": "u06-bazaar",
  "title": "Kilos, halis & vegetables",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Quantities go **number + measure + item**, with no 'of': **দুই কেজি আলু** 'two kilos (of) potatoes', **এক ডজন কলা** 'a dozen bananas'. Half a kilo is **আধা কেজি**; the Unit 5 fractions work too: **দেড় কেজি** 1½ kg, **আড়াই কেজি** 2½ kg.\n\nEggs and bananas are often sold by the **হালি** (*hali*), a set of **four**. **এক হালি ডিম** = 4 eggs. Ask for 'a dozen' and you may get three halis!\n\nTo say you **need** something, the person goes in the -র form and the verb is **লাগবে** (*lagbe*): **আমার পেঁয়াজ লাগবে** 'I need onions', literally 'to-me onions will-be-needed'. It's **আমার**, not আমি.\n\nBangladeshi vendors quote prices **per kilo**: **আলু পঞ্চাশ টাকা কেজি** 'potatoes are 50 taka a kilo'. To ask, say **টমেটোর কেজি কত?** 'How much is a kilo of tomatoes?'\n\n**Culture:** at the fresh market (*kacha bazar*) vegetables are weighed on a hand scale in front of you. It's fine to pick out your own tomatoes before they go on the scale.",
